  "worked_solution": "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{3}{5}.$ Also, note that $N=8,$ since there are $8$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=8{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =8{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{0-1} \\\\  & =8{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{-1} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=\\frac{40}{3}\\,,r=\\frac{3}{5}$ and $N=8$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{8} & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{8}]}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{8}]}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{3}\\cdot [1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{8}] \\\\  & \u224832.773\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places.",
